00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:21.740
Here is a voicemail about an issue that also
I look for forward to opportunities to clarify

00:00:21.740 --> 00:00:23.329
on other programs.

00:00:23.329 --> 00:00:27.650
And it is about whether the U.S. is the worst
or not on our coronavirus response.

00:00:27.650 --> 00:00:29.210
Take a listen.

00:00:29.210 --> 00:00:33.080
We obviously we have the worst record of anybody.

00:00:33.080 --> 00:00:35.270
We have one third of the deaths.

00:00:35.270 --> 00:00:39.370
One third of the cases of the whole planet.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.430
There's no doubt that we're the worst.

00:00:42.430 --> 00:00:43.430
The world is absolutely no doubt.

00:00:43.430 --> 00:00:45.780
OK, so here's where I am on this.

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:51.870
I. I don't know that the United States coronavirus
response has been the worst in the world.

00:00:51.870 --> 00:00:56.990
And I've been careful not to say that it's
been one of the worst.

00:00:56.990 --> 00:01:02.790
And if you say who who had the worst response
is slowest, most propagandistic suppressing

00:01:02.790 --> 00:01:07.050
data, not testing, you know, all this different
stuff from the countries we know of that have

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:08.520
had a bunch of cases.

00:01:08.520 --> 00:01:11.840
The United States is certainly on the list
of the worst.

00:01:11.840 --> 00:01:15.439
Brazil is on the list of the worst.

00:01:15.439 --> 00:01:18.270
India seems to be on that list.

00:01:18.270 --> 00:01:20.530
Russia is on the list of the worst responses.

00:01:20.530 --> 00:01:26.270
You know, I've heard anecdotally that Turkmenistan
had a terribly propagandistic totalitarian

00:01:26.270 --> 00:01:27.270
response.

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:28.659
I just don't know enough about that.

00:01:28.659 --> 00:01:29.899
And it's a very small country.

00:01:29.899 --> 00:01:34.049
So, you know, it can be difficult sometimes
to glean everything we want to glean.

00:01:34.049 --> 00:01:35.819
North Korea is thrown around.

00:01:35.819 --> 00:01:38.960
But here's where I think we need to shift
the conversation on this.

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:43.840
Instead of arguing, is the U.S. the worst
or are we like fifth worst or tenth worst

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:45.439
or 20th worst?

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:47.439
What happened to American exceptionalism?

00:01:47.439 --> 00:01:53.210
What happened to the people who say and believe
the U.S. should be the best that everything.

00:01:53.210 --> 00:01:55.530
We certainly were not the best.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:01:57.979
And we weren't second best or third best or
fourth best.

00:01:57.979 --> 00:02:05.549
So let's shift the debate from are we really
the worst or just bad to why are we now using

00:02:05.549 --> 00:02:06.840
that as a standard?

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:11.840
Since when is the standard in the United States
from those who subscribe to the idea of American

00:02:11.840 --> 00:02:13.170
exceptionalism?

00:02:13.170 --> 00:02:14.959
Since when is the standard?

00:02:14.959 --> 00:02:16.760
Have we avoided being the worse?

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.159
The standard has always been.

00:02:19.159 --> 00:02:20.519
Are we the best?

00:02:20.519 --> 00:02:23.310
And no one can argue that we are the best.

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:33.060
Merely looking at South Korea, Taiwan, Japan,
Greece, you know, even a bunch of European

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:34.060
countries.

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:35.870
We're certainly not in the top 10.

00:02:35.870 --> 00:02:40.739
I would argue we are in the worst 10 and all
of a sudden the debate is, are we are we the

00:02:40.739 --> 00:02:41.739
worst?

00:02:41.739 --> 00:02:43.170
That was never the debate.

00:02:43.170 --> 00:02:48.980
If you believe in American exceptionalism,
do not let them hijack that conversation.

